        {
            "name": "WebSocket Gameplay (manual)",
            "description": "Postman's native WebSocket request type isn't reliably exportable into a portable collection JSON, so this folder documents the steps instead of scripting them. Do this after running every request in 'REST Setup' above, in order. Needs all 3 players — RoomService.start_game rejects with STATE_CONFLICT ('Need at least 3 players to start game') below that.\n\n**1. Open three WebSocket requests in Postman** (New → WebSocket Request, one per player):\n\n- Host:    `ws://localhost:8000/ws/rooms/{{room_id}}?token={{u1_access}}`\n- Guest 1: `ws://localhost:8000/ws/rooms/{{room_id}}?token={{u2_access}}`\n- Guest 2: `ws://localhost:8000/ws/rooms/{{room_id}}?token={{u3_access}}`\n\nConnect all three. Each should immediately receive `room.connected` then `room.state_sync` in the messages pane. Note the `hand` array in each player's own `room.state_sync` payload — those are raw white-card ids, no text; cross-reference against `GET /cards/default?page_size=100` if you want to read what they say.\n\n**2. Start the game — send from the HOST socket:**\n```json\n{\"event\": \"lobby.start_game\", \"payload\": {}}\n```\nAll three sockets receive `game.started` then `round.started`. Copy the `round_id` and check `czar_id` — the host is czar round 1 (first czar is chosen deterministically), so the two guests are the ones submitting.\n\n**3. Submit cards — send from BOTH non-czar sockets** (guest 1 and guest 2, one card each, matching the black card's `pick` count):\n```json\n{\"event\": \"game.submit_cards\", \"payload\": {\"round_id\": \"<round_id from step 2>\", \"white_card_ids\": [\"<a card id from that player's own hand>\"]}}\n```\nAfter the *first* non-czar submission, all three sockets get `round.submission_accepted` (submitted_count 1, required_count 2) — the round isn't complete yet. Only after the *second* non-czar player submits does `round.judging` broadcast, with the anonymized, shuffled `submissions` array. Copy a `submission_id` from it.\n\n**4. Pick the winner — send from the HOST socket** (host is czar this round):\n```json\n{\"event\": \"game.pick_winner\", \"payload\": {\"round_id\": \"<same round_id>\", \"submission_id\": \"<submission_id from step 3>\"}}\n```\nAll three sockets receive `round.result`, then either another `round.started` (repeat steps 3-4 — czar rotates to one of the guests, so the submitting pair changes each round) or `game.over` once someone's score reaches the room's `winning_score` (3, per the create-room request above).\n\nWhile doing this, keep `docker compose logs -f backend` open in a terminal — WebSocket traffic doesn't produce access-log lines the way REST calls do, so watching the SQL/Redis calls scroll by as you send each message is the best way to see the protocol happen for real.",
            "item": []
        }
    ],
    "variable": [
        { "key": "baseUrl", "value": "http://localhost:8000" }
    ]
